  <!-- ── Why this matters ── -->
  <h2 id="why-matters">Why This Matters</h2>
  <ul class="insult-list">
    <li>Cognitive decline is reversible when caught early — the brain follows a ruthless use-it-or-lose-it principle</li>
    <li>Chronic sleep debt, untreated gut inflammation, and hormonal decline are silently degrading neural architecture right now</li>
    <li>Mind is the destination of the entire 4M framework — everything else feeds into cognitive output</li>
  </ul>

  <aside class="gut-cognitive-bridge">
    <h3>Your cognition is downstream of your gut</h3>
    <p>Before you reach for a nootropic, three numbers worth knowing:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>~90% of your serotonin</strong> — mood, sleep, appetite — is made in your gut, not your brain.</li>
      <li><strong>~50% of your dopamine</strong> — motivation, reward, focus — is made there too.</li>
      <li><strong>The vagus nerve carries 90% of its traffic gut-to-brain</strong>, not the other way around. Brain fog usually starts in the gut and arrives in the head days later.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>This is why the 4M sequence is gut first, then sleep, then cognitive optimization. NeuroBridge gives the brain the methylated building blocks it needs — but if the gut isn't sealed, the upstream signal is still inflammatory. <a href="/solutions/gut">See the gut protocol →</a></p>
  </aside>

  <MiniTwoPaths otcLabel="Order NeuroBridge" otcHref="/assessment" prompt="Ready to act?" />

  <aside class="research-callout">
    <h3>What the research actually says — creatine for cognition</h3>
    <p class="research-callout-intro">Most "premium nootropic" stacks ($60–100/month) are built on Bacopa, Lion's Mane, and proprietary blends with weak RCT support. The most-evidenced cognitive ingredient in the published literature is one most men only think of for the gym.</p>
    <ul class="research-list">
      <li>
        <strong>5g/day creatine for 6 weeks improved working memory and intelligence test performance.</strong> Double-blind, placebo-controlled, crossover trial. Working memory improved (P=.003); Raven's Advanced Progressive Matrices improved (P&lt;.0001). <span class="research-source">Rae et al., <em>Proc. Royal Society B</em>, 2003. <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/14561278/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PubMed 14561278</a>.</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A single 25g creatine dose improved cognitive performance under sleep deprivation within 3 hours.</strong> Magnetic resonance spectroscopy showed measurable increases in cerebral high-energy phosphates. Brain phosphocreatine isn't theoretical — it's measurable, and it changes cognitive output. <span class="research-source">Gordji-Nejad et al., <em>Scientific Reports</em>, 2024. <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38418482/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PubMed 38418482</a>.</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Beetroot juice (430mg dietary nitrate) increased cerebral blood flow during cognitive task performance</strong> and improved performance on demanding mental arithmetic. Cerebral perfusion declines ~0.4–0.5% per year in adulthood — improving endothelial NO is one of the few directly-actionable levers. <span class="research-source">Wightman et al., <em>Physiology &amp; Behavior</em>, 2015. <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26037632/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PubMed 26037632</a>.</span>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="research-callout-footnote"><em>This is the underrated cognitive longevity story: the most-published nootropic ingredient in the peer-reviewed literature also happens to be the most-published lean-mass preservation ingredient. One supplement. Same physiology.</em></p>
  </aside>

  <MiniTwoPaths otcLabel="Order NeuroBridge" otcHref="/assessment" prompt="Pick your path:" />

  <!-- ── In development: MitoVita ── -->
  <aside class="product-in-dev">
    <span class="product-in-dev-badge">In development — Q4 2026</span>
    <h3>MitoVita</h3>
    <p class="product-in-dev-tagline"><em>The four-pillar mitochondrial supplement most men have never heard of.</em></p>
    <p>Once-daily formula: <strong>creatine monohydrate (5g)</strong>, an <strong>NO-boosting blend</strong> (L-citrulline + standardized beetroot extract), and a <strong>functional electrolyte matrix</strong>. Sweetened with monk fruit only — fast-compatible.</p>
    <p><strong>One mechanism, four outcomes.</strong> Creatine is a mitochondrial ATP buffer; the NO blend delivers oxygen to those mitochondria via vasodilation. Mitochondrial function under adequate blood flow is the upstream node behind every age-related decline our demographic faces: cognitive function, lean-mass preservation, visceral-fat reduction, and sexual performance. Most men have heard of creatine for the gym. Almost none have heard about creatine for working memory, intelligence test performance, and resilience to sleep-deprived cognitive decline — all published in peer-reviewed research.</p>
    <p class="product-in-dev-note">Formulation under clinical advisory board review. Manufacturing partner selection in progress.</p>
  </aside>

  <aside class="four-m-touchpoints">
    <h3>How cognitive support touches all 4 Ms</h3>
    <div class="four-m-grid">
      <div class="four-m-card"><span class="four-m-label">Mind</span><p>Bioavailable B-vitamins your brain actually uses to make serotonin, dopamine, and GABA — the chemicals behind mood, focus, and calm. Creatine adds working memory and intelligence support on top.</p></div>
      <div class="four-m-card"><span class="four-m-label">Muscle</span><p>B-vitamins convert food into ATP — the energy your muscles run on. Creatine is the most-evidenced ingredient for lean-mass preservation past 40.</p></div>
      <div class="four-m-card"><span class="four-m-label">Mitigate</span><p>Methylated B-complex clears homocysteine — a top independent risk factor for heart disease and dementia. NO support from beetroot and citrulline maintains cerebral blood flow.</p></div>
      <div class="four-m-card"><span class="four-m-label">Motivate</span><p>Steady energy. Freedom from the low-grade fatigue and brain fog men accept as "just getting older."</p></div>
    </div>
    <div class="lifetime-usage">
      <h4>Why you'll want this for life</h4>
      <p>Up to 60% of people carry genetic variants (MTHFR) that block standard B-vitamin conversion — a permanent feature of your biology. PPIs, metformin, and alcohol all deplete B-vitamins faster than diet replaces them. Cerebral blood flow declines about 0.4–0.5% per year in adulthood. Your body's ability to make creatine drops as you age, and dietary intake alone doesn't replace what your brain needs. The men who stop supporting these systems past 50 are accepting a decline trajectory they didn't have to accept.</p>
    </div>
  </aside>

  <MiniTwoPaths otcLabel="Order NeuroBridge" otcHref="/assessment" prompt="The 4M decision:" />

